rovide my impact  where do I keep the course of humans  living in space on track we were asking  that question why aren't people living  on the moon right now why don't we have  a base on Mars while we could have gone  and looked at all the different ways to  make a rocket better we said you know  what if instead of making better rockets  we looked at a way to not need rockets  to state the obvious going to space is  really expensive right now it costs  around $10,000 per pound to launch  payload into outer space there currently  are a treasure trove worth of spare  parts on the International Space Station  they have backups and then backups for  backups lots of weight lots of space  over a billion dollars worth of parts  most of which will never be used they're  there just in case you can't do that  going to Mars it's not cost-effective  you can't afford that  and here's where we come to the  breakthrough idea what if we could make  all those spare parts and tools in outer  space enter the 3d printer so this is  where the magic happens we're doing a  cribs episode earlier so this is we call  this the bullpen this is a where pretty  much most the people like engineers I'll  work this is our latest album cover iron  made in space a lot of 3d printed stuff  if you look around this is Dylan and  this is Dylan I actually have one at  home that didn't finish printing the  heads like half cut off it's kind of  creepy joking aside it's one thing to  talk about making a 3d printer for use  in space but to build it that's another  thing entirely how do you develop a 3d  printer for the space station without  even knowing if current 3d printers  could work in space can they work in  weightlessness so what we did is we went  to NASA and we told them the plan and we  said if we could fly some 3d printers on  a zero-gravity airplane and if they can  work they should be in space the first  real breakthrough for the company was  when we actually you know built to build  what we thought was a gravity  independent 3d printing technology and  then we're able to deploy that on on  that parabolic aircraft which has the  somewhat unfortunate name with the Vomit  Comet and and actually experience it  working yeah 2001 was the first  parabolic flights and you know when we  first my first saw the results of our  work I was really I was really happy  because we built something that not only  function but now function in the  environment we wanted to function and a  lot of people always say you know oh I'm  working on this or we've been working on  this since you know 2000 and X we're not  working on it we're just doing it in  2011 they got a printer to work in zero  gravity but that was only step one step  to get a printer on the international  space station  as soon as we figured out how to make a  printer work in zero gravity by doing  these research flights on the airplane  it took us almost exactly three years to  get to the point where we have a printer  ready for the space station launch is a  scary time and here's why I had nothing  to do with that rocket I don't know if  somebody had a bad day at work and  decided not to do their job and you know  threw a wrench somewhere it shouldn't be  that's the only part of the mission that  we didn't have anything to do it  it was so tense leading up to that first  time the printer turned on we had tested  so many times and we knew how well it  would work but we're still tense like  getting it set up getting installed and  then then comes a moment to finally like  turn it on and operate it and it works  that moment marked the first time in the  history of humanity that mankind had  manufactured anything off the face of  planet Earth where we headed right now  we're headed into the conference room we  always keep this on display and it's  this is one of the prototypes of the  printer that's on the space station so  this is a good good example of a tool  that we've built in space and this  actually came from the request of an  astronaut asking for a ratchet and we we  designed this ratchet from literally  from nothing and had it printed in space  five days later  the ability to manufacture objects you  know off face the planet do do  manufacturing in space rather than  having to rely on everything to be  shipped up in a in a predefined shape is  is a fundamental shift in how we would  do things in space and it really enables  us to go you know that much further so  the big question is what's next for  maiden space well in March of 2016 they  sent a new print of the space station  but they've got much much bigger plans  than that they've already started  talking about making printers that could  print using moon or Martian deaths sure  it sounds like science fiction but come  on look with this scrappy gang of thirty  year olds with nothing more than a dream  accomplished in just six short years  when I was in college I interned for  NASA and I did it because I loved space  I loved NASA and I really wanted to to  make a difference there but at the end  of the day I always felt like I was  working to help somebody else achieve  their dream and instead of spending my  time helping somebody else achieve their  dream I could spend my time working to  achieve my own you really need to find  meaning and value out of what you're  doing with your own life  [Music]  [Music]
 

